          "text": "{\"translation_en\":\"“Oh! It’s because **I love you**!” she went on. “I love you so much that I cannot do without you, do you understand? Sometimes I long to see you again so fiercely that all the furies of love tear me apart. I ask myself, ‘Where is he? Perhaps he is talking to other women? They smile at him, he draws closer…’ Oh! No—none of them attracts you, do they? There are women more beautiful than I am; but I know how to love better! I am your servant and your concubine! You are my king, my idol! You are kind! You are handsome! You are intelligent! You are strong!”\",\"larger_context_summary\":\"Emma Bovary is addressing her lover Rodolphe Boulanger during their affair. Dissatisfied with her marriage to Charles and steeped in romantic fantasies, she treats the illicit relationship as an all-consuming passion and casts Rodolphe in extravagantly idealized terms. Here she seeks reassurance about other women while proclaiming dependence, jealousy, and submission. The larger plot makes the imbalance important: Emma invests the affair with hopes of absolute devotion and escape, whereas the more calculating and experienced Rodolphe does not share her romantic conception of their bond. Her emotional and practical investment therefore carries serious personal and social risks.\",\"interpretive_commentary\":\"The highlighted words function chiefly as a direct avowal of Emma’s emotional state. Their strong truth-presenting force is reinforced by the immediate repetition and by concrete elaborations—she says she cannot live without Rodolphe, suffers jealous longing, and regards him as an idol. The speech is certainly intense, but intensity is not the same as reflexive or discharge-like expressive force: “she went on” introduces a sustained, rhetorically organized profession rather than an involuntary cry. There is a limited performative dimension because Emma’s language of servitude and concubinage presents her as yielding herself to the relationship, yet the highlighted declaration itself does not clearly promise a future action or create an obligation. The wider novel may make readers doubt the stability, realism, or reciprocity of this passion, but such dramatic irony does not erase the core semantic act: Emma presents her love as true.\"}"
        }
